00:30:21.930 --> 00:30:27.869
Kent Dybvig: Number of… that occupy more bytes than the number of bytes that fit in a field.

228
00:30:28.470 --> 00:30:30.669
Kent Dybvig: But occupy their abyss.

229
00:30:30.800 --> 00:30:34.530
Kent Dybvig: Than the number of bits that fit in a field.

230
00:30:35.380 --> 00:30:43.290
Kent Dybvig: Right now, as far as I can… as far as I understand, the,

231
00:30:44.070 --> 00:30:53.810
Kent Dybvig: the descriptors that we use in TypeScript to, to convert between ledger and, compact types, that is.

232
00:30:54.370 --> 00:30:59.980
Kent Dybvig: really, TypeScript types, or JavaScript types for,

233
00:31:00.250 --> 00:31:15.019
Kent Dybvig: or unsigned integers, ends up saying that, well, that must take up two fields, because it takes up more bytes. It's measured in bytes instead of bits. And so it says, oh, it takes up more bytes than fit in a field, therefore this must take up two fields.

234
00:31:15.400 --> 00:31:21.660
Kent Dybvig: And that causes, of course, a proof failure, because the, the…

235
00:31:21.880 --> 00:31:26.759
Kent Dybvig: the CKR code, knows that these things should only occupy one field.

236
00:31:28.070 --> 00:31:40.159
Kent Dybvig: So the question is, how do we… how do we fix this? And, you know, the simplest fix in the world would be just to say, okay, well, the maximum unsigned integer is the one that fits in the number of field bytes.

237
00:31:41.970 --> 00:31:48.739
Kent Dybvig: So we don't even allow those ones that don't fit in the number of field bytes, even if they would fit in the number of field bits.

238
00:31:49.300 --> 00:32:00.629
Kent Dybvig: So that's… that's one possibility. Another possibility is that we, simply just use the field descriptor for unsigned integers.

239
00:32:01.830 --> 00:32:09.919
Kent Dybvig: And so the question is, why don't we just use the field descriptor for unsigned integers? Are we getting some advantage off of

