00:32:20.280 --> 00:32:28.509
Thomas Kerber: Yes. So… Do we get an advantage of some other descriptor?

244
00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:34.780
Thomas Kerber: Yes, so one thing that it does get us to use something else than the field descriptor there.

245
00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:41.600
Thomas Kerber: Is, if we store it in… In the ledger state.

246
00:32:41.800 --> 00:32:47.119
Thomas Kerber: Then, that descriptor gets stored in there as well.

247
00:32:47.430 --> 00:32:54.220
Thomas Kerber: And in particular, that means that down the line, if we ever changed our field size.

248
00:32:54.870 --> 00:32:59.029
Thomas Kerber: We would know whether it is affected by that change or not.

249
00:32:59.390 --> 00:33:04.599
Thomas Kerber: So if, for example, we ever decided we're going to cut our field size in half.

250
00:33:05.100 --> 00:33:08.989
Thomas Kerber: Which may happen, it may also not.

251
00:33:09.440 --> 00:33:17.160
Thomas Kerber: Then, if we see, oh, this is a… You know, 8-byte object.

252
00:33:17.450 --> 00:33:21.739
Thomas Kerber: We know it doesn't care, or even if we see that it's a…

253
00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:28.210
Thomas Kerber: object that is, you know, up to or longer than the field size. We know that it's a… something…

254
00:33:28.570 --> 00:33:32.529
Thomas Kerber: Fundamentally is the number of bytes that we will interpret differently

255
00:33:32.700 --> 00:33:37.500
Thomas Kerber: As, say, a big int in whatever the new thing is, for example.

256
00:33:37.800 --> 00:33:40.739
Thomas Kerber: Whereas, if we're storing it as a field element.

257
00:33:40.920 --> 00:33:48.420
Thomas Kerber: We kind of have to just heuristically say it's probably still going to be an integer

258
00:33:48.610 --> 00:33:50.919
Thomas Kerber: Hopefully still in range.

259
00:33:51.090 --> 00:33:58.650
Thomas Kerber: So, I think there is… Yeah, I think it is relatively important that that information does survive.

